Here’s a list of what’s wrong in the snippet and concrete ways to fix or improve it:

1. Bug—wrong escaping in the string literal  
   - In JavaScript string literals, “\s” is *not* a valid escape sequence, so the engine quietly turns `\s` into just `s`.  
   - As written  
     ```js
     new RegExp('^https?:\/\/[^\s]+$')
     ```  
     actually becomes the pattern  
     ```
     ^https?://[^s]+$
     ```  
     which means “http(s):// followed by one or more characters that are *not* the letter “s”.”  
   - Consequence: whitespace is allowed, the letter “s” is *forbidden*, you’re not actually checking what you think you are.

   Fixes:  
   a) If you stick with the `RegExp` constructor, you must double-escape backslashes:  
   ```js
   function isValidURL(str) {
     // note the \\s
     const pattern = new RegExp('^https?://[^\\s]+$');
     return pattern.test(str);
   }
   ```  
   b) Or even clearer—use a regex literal (no string un-escaping headaches):  
   ```js
   function isValidURL(str) {
     // literal, so \s really means whitespace
     return /^(https?:\/\/)[^\s]+$/.test(str);
   }
   ```

2. Security / robustness—this regex is still *way* too simple  
   - It allows URLs like `http://example.com\n<script>…` (because `\s` is only one class, and your own literal could still be too permissive if you fix the escape).  
   - It allows invalid hosts (`http://-foo.com`, `http://999.999.999.999`), unbounded length, etc.  
   - It is vulnerable to ReDoS if you ever expand it without care.

   Better approach—use the built-in URL parser:  
   ```js
   function isValidHTTPURL(str) {
     try {
       const url = new URL(str);
       // only accept http or https
       return url.protocol === 'http:' || url.protocol === 'https:';
     } catch (err) {
       return false;
     }
   }
   ```  
   Pros:  
   • The browser’s URL parser handles all edge cases (punycode, IPv6, port ranges, username/password in URL, etc.).  
   • It rejects most invalid constructs for you.  
   • You avoid any hand-rolled regex problems.

3. Open-redirect & injection considerations  
   - If you ever do `window.location = userInput` or output it into HTML without proper escaping, a “valid” URL check by itself isn’t enough.  
   - Always combine this `isValidHTTPURL` check with context-appropriate escaping or whitelisting of domains if you’re doing redirects.  

4. Additional hardening  
   - If you really do need a regex, build it in pieces, anchor carefully, consider each URL component (host, port, path, query, fragment), and limit repetition to avoid ReDoS.  
   - Trim user input first: `str = str.trim()` so someone can’t slip in leading/trailing whitespace.  
   - Enforce a maximum overall length if you’re paranoid (e.g. reject URLs longer than 2 048 characters).  

Putting it all together, I’d recommend this final version:

```js
function isValidHTTPURL(str) {
  if (typeof str !== 'string') return false;
  str = str.trim();
  if (str.length === 0 || str.length > 2048) return false;

  try {
    const url = new URL(str);
    return url.protocol === 'http:' || url.protocol === 'https:';
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}
```

This will correctly reject malformed inputs, only allow http/https, and leverage the browser’s own URL‐parsing logic rather than a brittle hand-rolled regex.
